{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"tl-v1","tool":"ragflow_retrieval","arguments":{"dataset_ids":["74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37"],"metadata_condition":{"logic":"and","conditions":[{"name":"path","comparison_operator":"is","value":"sources"}]},"page_size":10,"vector_similarity_weight":0.3,"similarity_threshold":0.2,"top_k":1024,"keyword":false,"question":"dmathieu response final decision close 8014 current behavior correct parent middleware accessing span context reverted after middleware execution pass current context to spawned goroutines"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"chunks\": [{\"content\": \"**Source ID**: [SRC-NOTE-04]\\n\\n# Meeting Note: OpenTelemetry Go SIG — 2025-11-03\\n\\n**Canonical URL**: https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go/issues/6648#issuecomment-3479584781  \\n\\n**Author**: pellared  \\n\\n**Created**: 2025-11-03T09:18:08Z · **Updated**: 2025-11-03T09:18:08Z  \\n\\n**Collected**: 2026-07-14T14:35:41Z\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Date:** 2025-10-16\\n\\n**Attendees:**\\n\\n*   Tyler Yahn\\n\\n*   Damien Mathieu\\n\\n*   Bryan Boreham\\n\\n*   David Ashpole\\n\\n*   Robert Pająk\\n\\n## Transcript\\n\\n**1. otelgin Context Reversal in DataRace**\\n\\n*   **Damien Mathieu** raised an issue regarding context reversal in DataRace for otelgin. The context, stored within the otelgin struct, is reverted after middleware execution to prevent parent middlewares from accessing the span. However, Go routines spawned after a request might still need the span in their context. This discussion was based on [https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/pull/8014](https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/pull/8014).\\n\\n*   Damien believes the current behavior is correct and that users should explicitly pass the current context to spawned Go routines. He cited the collector's pattern of passing context directly outside HTTP requests for processors and exporters as a good example.\\n\\n*   **Tyler Yahn** agreed, suggesting that passing necessary values like the span to spawned Go routines is the appropriate solution. He also mentioned OpenTelemetry's pattern of linking spans for asynchronous operations rather than making them child references.\\n\\n**2. otelgin Middleware and Temporary Multi-part Forms**\\n\\n*   **Damien Mathieu** brought up an issue where using middleware causes temporary multi-part forms not to be destroyed. This occurs because the instrumentation clones the HTTP request, but the multipart form is not duplicated, preventing proper cleanup. This issue is a historical one, manifesting in otelgin but potentially affecting virtually all HTTP-related middleware.\\n\\n*   References for this issue include:\\n\\n    *   [https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/issues/5946](https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/issues/5946)\\n\\n    *   [https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/pull/6609](https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/pull/6609)\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"sourc id src note 04 meet note opentelemetri go sig 2025 11 03 canon url http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go issu 6648 issuecom 3479584781 author pellar creat 2025 11 03t09 18 08z updat 2025 11 03t09 18 08z collect 2026 07 14t14 35 41z date 2025 10 16 attende tyler yahn damien mathieu bryan boreham david ashpol robert paj ą k transcript 1 otelgin context revers in datarac damien mathieu rais an issu regard context revers in datarac for otelgin the context store within the otelgin struct is revert after middlewar execut to prevent parent middlewar from access the span howev go routin spawn after a request might still need the span in their context this discuss wa base on http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib pull 8014 http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib pull 8014 damien belief the current behavior is correct and that user should explicit pas the current context to spawn go routin he cite the collector s pattern of pass context direct outsid http request for processor and export a a good exampl tyler yahn agre suggest that pass necessari valu like the span to spawn go routin is the appropri solut he also mention opentelemetri s pattern of link span for asynchron oper rather than make them child refer 2 otelgin middlewar and temporari multi part form damien mathieu brought up an issu where use middlewar caus temporari multi part form not to be destroy this occur becaus the instrument clone the http request but the multipart form is not duplic prevent proper cleanup this issu is a histor one manifest in otelgin but potenti affect virtual all http relat middlewar refer for this issu includ http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib issu 5946 http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib issu 5946 http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib pull 6609 http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib pull 6609\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"873e60b08a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/meeting-notes/entry-3479584781.md\", \"id\": \"d2762ef7a24b09d4\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.353639897171124, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.19383071024446294, \"vector_similarity\": 0.726528, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/meeting-notes/entry-3479584781.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"873e60b08a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/meeting-notes/entry-3479584781.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/meeting-notes/entry-3479584781.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 8756, \"chunk_count\": 4, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:25\", \"token_count\": 2164, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"```go\\n\\nfunc Middleware() {\\n\\n    savedCtx := c.Request.Context()\\n\\n    defer func() {\\n\\n        c.Request = c.Request.WithContext(savedCtx)  // ← Step 5: WRITE\\n\\n    }()\\n\\n    c.Request = c.Request.WithContext(ctx)  // Step 1\\n\\n    c.Next()                                 // Step 2: All handlers/middleware run\\n\\n    // Step 3: Handler spawned goroutine that reads c.Request.Context()\\n\\n    // Step 4: Handler returns\\n\\n    // Step 5: defer runs and WRITES to c.Request\\n\\n    // Step 6: Background goroutine READS c.Request ← **RACE**!\\n\\n}\\n\\n```\\n\\nThe defer IS the concurrent modification. It writes to c.Request after handlers complete, while\\n\\nbackground goroutines spawned by those handlers may still be reading it.\\n\\nThe fix removes this write:\\n\\n```go\\n\\nfunc Middleware() {\\n\\n    reqCtx := c.Request.Context()\\n\\n    ctx := cfg.Propagators.Extract(reqCtx, ...)\\n\\n    c.Request = c.Request.WithContext(ctx)  // Step 1: Set once\\n\\n    c.Next()                                 // Step 2: Handlers run\\n\\n    // Step 3: Handler spawns goroutine\\n\\n    // Step 4: Handler returns\\n\\n    // Step 5: No defer, no write ← NO RACE\\n\\n}\\n\\n```\\n\\nProof: TestMiddleware_MinimalRaceRepro demonstrates exactly this:\\n\\n- Handler spawns goroutine that accesses c.Request.Context()\\n\\n- Handler returns\\n\\n- Old code: defer writes → DATA RACE\\n\\n- Fixed code: no write → PASS\\n\\nFurthermore this isn't my \\\"specific case\\\" - this is how Go middleware is supposed to work. The defer breaks standard Go patterns that every Go developer uses.\\n\\nGo's own documentation recommends this pattern\\n\\nFrom https://pkg.go.dev/context:\\n\\n>   \\\"Incoming requests to a server should create a Context, and outgoing calls to servers should accept a Context. The chain of function calls between them must propagate the Context,\\n\\n>   optionally replacing it with a derived Context...\\\"\\n\\n> \\n\\nPassing c.Request.Context() to goroutines is the recommended approach for:\\n\\n  - Timeouts\\n\\n  - Cancellation\\n\\n  - Tracing (which is what otelgin does!)\\n\\nOther popular Gin middleware use this exact pattern!\\n\\nExample using an extremely common pattern in production:\\n\\n```go\\n\\n func main() {\\n\\n      router := gin.New()\\n\\n      // Setup OpenTelemetry tracing (standard pattern)\\n\\n      router.Use(otelgin.Middleware(\\\"my-service\\\"))\\n\\n      // Add async logging middleware (also standard pattern)\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"go func middlewar savedctx c request context defer func c request c request withcontext savedctx step 5 write c request c request withcontext ctx step 1 c next step 2 all handler middlewar run step 3 handler spawn goroutin that read c request context step 4 handler return step 5 defer run and write to c request step 6 background goroutin read c request race the defer is the concurr modif it write to c request after handler complet while background goroutin spawn by those handler may still be read it the fix remov this write go func middlewar reqctx c request context ctx cfg propag extract reqctx c request c request withcontext ctx step 1 set onc c next step 2 handler run step 3 handler spawn goroutin step 4 handler return step 5 no defer no write no race proof testmiddlewar _ minimalracerepro demonstr exact this handler spawn goroutin that access c request context handler return old code defer write data race fix code no write pas furthermor this isn t my specif case this is how go middlewar is suppos to work the defer break standard go pattern that everi go develop us go s own document recommend this pattern from http pkg go dev context incom request to a server should creat a context and outgo call to server should accept a context the chain of function call between them must propag the context option replac it with a deriv context pass c request context to goroutin is the recommend approach for timeout cancel trace which is what otelgin doe other popular gin middlewar use this exact pattern exampl use an extrem common pattern in product go func main router gin new setup opentelemetri trace standard pattern router use otelgin middlewar my servic add async log middlewar also standard pattern\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"f31afd660670eb11\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.2960994498483038, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.09202387121186258, \"vector_similarity\": 0.7722758, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"> \\\"Contexts form a tree... Successive calls to WithValue, WithCancel, WithDeadline, or WithTimeout derive new contexts.\\\"\\n\\nContexts are meant to flow forward, not be \\\"restored\\\" backward. The library shouldn't enforce this opinion.\\n\\n2. Most users want otelgin early in the chain\\n\\nI (and I assume most users) place otelgin as one of the first middleware specifically so child spans can be created from it:\\n\\nr.Use(otelgin.Middleware(\\\"my-service\\\"))  // ← First - creates root span\\n\\nr.Use(loggingMiddleware)                  // ← Creates child spans\\n\\nr.Use(authMiddleware)                     // ← Creates child spans\\n\\nr.GET(\\\"/\\\", handler)                       // ← Handler creates child spans\\n\\nCommon use cases that need the parent span:\\n\\n- Database transactions with tracing\\n\\n- Inter-service HTTP calls with trace propagation\\n\\n- Structured logging with trace IDs\\n\\n- Background jobs that need trace context\\n\\nYour example breaks all of these - they'd have no parent span.\\n\\n3. Users can control span isolation themselves \\n\\nIf a user doesn't want their spans to be children of otelgin's span, they can do exactly what the defer does - but explicitly:\\n\\n```go\\n\\nvar tracer = otel.Tracer(instrumentationName)\\n\\nfunc myCustomMiddleware(c *gin.Context) {\\n\\n    ctx, span := tracer.Start(c.Request.Context(), \\\"myCustomMiddleware\\\")\\n\\n    defer span.End()\\n\\n    c.Next() // runs otelgin and other middleware\\n\\n    // Explicitly restore context if the user doesn't want child spans and since the user is in control of goroutines the onus is on them to do so safely (as it should be)  \\n\\n    c.Request = c.Request.WithContext(ctx)\\n\\n}\\n\\n```\\n\\nThis gives users control without the library making opinionated (and race-prone) decisions.\\n\\nEven if we agreed on the semantics, I'm not sure of a safe way of doing it from a library implementation.\\n\\nBottom line: The defer enforces an anti-pattern, breaks the common use case (otelgin as root span), prevents valid patterns (goroutines with trace context), and causes data races.\\n\\nUsers who need span isolation can manage it themselves without the library making unsafe assumptions.\\n\\nWhat do you think? I'm happy to add documentation showing how users can achieve span isolation if they need it. \\n\\n---\\n\\n**dmathieu** — 2025-10-14T08:42:01Z\\n\\nI disagree with this being a violation of Go's conventions (in the context of the middleware pattern, we don't flow forward. At the end of a middleware, we go back to its parent).\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"context form a tree success call to withvalu withcancel withdeadlin or withtimeout deriv new context context are meant to flow forward not be restor backward the librari shouldn t enforc this opinion 2 most user want otelgin earli in the chain i and i assum most user place otelgin a one of the first middlewar specif so child span can be creat from it r use otelgin middlewar my servic first creat root span r use loggingmiddlewar creat child span r use authmiddlewar creat child span r get handler handler creat child span common use case that need the parent span databas transact with trace inter servic http call with trace propag structur log with trace id background job that need trace context your exampl break all of these they d have no parent span 3 user can control span isol themselv if a user doesn t want their span to be child of otelgin s span they can do exact what the defer doe but explicit go var tracer otel tracer instrumentationnam func mycustommiddlewar c gin context ctx span tracer start c request context mycustommiddlewar defer span end c next run otelgin and other middlewar explicit restor context if the user doesn t want child span and sinc the user is in control of goroutin the onus is on them to do so safe a it should be c request c request withcontext ctx this give user control without the librari make opinion and race prone decis even if we agre on the semant i m not sure of a safe way of do it from a librari implement bottom line the defer enforc an anti pattern break the common use case otelgin a root span prevent valid pattern goroutin with trace context and caus data race user who need span isol can manag it themselv without the librari make unsaf assumpt what do you think i m happi to add document show how user can achiev span isol if they need it dmathieu 2025 10 14t08 42 01z i disagre with this be a violat of go s convent in the context of the middlewar pattern we don t flow forward at the end of a middlewar we go back to it parent\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"9ae5e4f55cbf7762\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.291189210470081, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.08149212924297289, \"vector_similarity\": 0.7804824, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"I'm not quite sure about your specific case, so this is for reference only: Since `*http.Request` is often passed to subsequent logic, if there are concurrent modifications to it, it can lead to thread safety issues.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**akats7** — 2025-10-14T05:13:28Z\\n\\n> > I'm confused about this \\\"doesn't provide thread safety\\\" claim. I'm starring at a test failure due to data race when running `go test -race` in one of my projects that clearly points the finger to the code I removed as being the culprit. When I do a `go mod -replace` using my change in this PR the race goes away.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> I'm not quite sure about your specific case, so this is for reference only: Since `*http.Request` is often passed to subsequent logic, if there are concurrent modifications to it, it can lead to thread safety issues.\\n\\n That’s essentially what’s happening here, this would be an example of subsequent logic that’s creating a data race. We can’t guarantee that others middleware’s won’t do something similar but I think this is a valid fix.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**nicksherron** — 2025-10-14T05:13:48Z\\n\\n  The \\\"hygiene to avoid leaking modifications\\\" argument is backwards. The defer causes the race, it doesn't prevent leaking:\\n\\n  1. There's nothing to leak - Gin doesn't reuse *gin.Context across requests. Each request gets a fresh context.\\n\\n  2. The defer IS the race - When a handler spawns a goroutine that reads c.Request.Context(), the defer writes to c.Request while that goroutine reads from it. That's the data race.\\n\\n  3. Proof:\\n\\n  ```go\\n\\n  router.POST(\\\"/test\\\", func(c *gin.Context) {\\n\\n      ctx := c.Request.Context()\\n\\n      go func() {\\n\\n          time.Sleep(1ms)\\n\\n          _ = ctx.Value(\\\"key\\\")  // READ\\n\\n      }()\\n\\n      c.JSON(200, \\\"ok\\\")\\n\\n      // defer runs here and WRITES to c.Request ← RACE!\\n\\n  })\\n\\n  ```\\n\\nThe \\\"concurrent modifications\\\" concern is the problem we're fixing - The defer itself is the concurrent modification! It writes to c.Request after the handler returns while background goroutines may still be using it.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**nicksherron** — 2025-10-14T05:19:18Z\\n\\n Here's a test you can run to test this for yourself. It proves the current implementation is the culprit for data races.\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"i m not quit sure about your specif case so this is for refer onli sinc http request is often pass to subsequ logic if there are concurr modif to it it can lead to thread safeti issu akats7 2025 10 14t05 13 28z i m confus about this doesn t provid thread safeti claim i m star at a test failur due to data race when run go test race in one of my project that clear point the finger to the code i remov a be the culprit when i do a go mod replac use my chang in this pr the race go away i m not quit sure about your specif case so this is for refer onli sinc http request is often pass to subsequ logic if there are concurr modif to it it can lead to thread safeti issu that s essenti what s happen here this would be an exampl of subsequ logic that s creat a data race we can t guarante that other middlewar s won t do someth similar but i think this is a valid fix nicksherron 2025 10 14t05 13 48z the hygien to avoid leak modif argument is backward the defer caus the race it doesn t prevent leak 1 there s noth to leak gin doesn t reus gin context across request each request get a fresh context 2 the defer is the race when a handler spawn a goroutin that read c request context the defer write to c request while that goroutin read from it that s the data race 3 proof go router post test func c gin context ctx c request context go func time sleep 1ms _ ctx valu key read c json 200 ok defer run here and write to c request race the concurr modif concern is the problem we re fix the defer itself is the concurr modif it write to c request after the handler return while background goroutin may still be use it nicksherron 2025 10 14t05 19 18z here s a test you can run to test this for yourself it prof the current implement is the culprit for data race\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"70a672b21a8b9d30\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.291119452767119, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.0990450325244557, \"vector_similarity\": 0.7392931, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"**Labels**: —  \\n\\n**Milestone**: —\\n\\n---\\n\\n## The Defer Provides No Benefit\\n\\nLet's analyze what the current code does:\\n\\n```go\\n\\n  savedCtx := c.Request.Context()\\n\\n  defer func() {\\n\\n      c.Request = c.Request.WithContext(savedCtx)  // ← Restores AFTER request completes\\n\\n  }()\\n\\n  ctx := cfg.Propagators.Extract(savedCtx, propagation.HeaderCarrier(c.Request.Header))\\n\\n  // ...\\n\\n  c.Request = c.Request.WithContext(ctx)  // ← Sets new context\\n\\n  c.Next()  // ← Processes request\\n\\n  // When we return, defer runs and \\\"restores\\\" the old context\\n\\n```\\n\\n  Question: Why restore the context after the request is complete?\\n\\n  - After c.Next() returns, the request has been fully processed\\n\\n  - Gin doesn't reuse *gin.Context instances across requests\\n\\n  - Downstream handlers may spawn goroutines that read c.Request.Context()\\n\\n  - The deferred write to c.Request races with those goroutine reads\\n\\n  - There is no code that benefits from this restoration\\n\\n**Source ID**: [SRC-PR-07]\\n\\n# Pull Request: otelgin: remove unneeded context save/restore to avoid data race (open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib#8014)\\n\\n**Canonical URL**: https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/pull/8014  \\n\\n**Author**: nicksherron  \\n\\n**Created**: 2025-10-14T04:01:24Z · **Updated**: 2025-10-17T10:42:07Z  \\n\\n**Collected**: 2026-07-14T14:35:41Z  \\n\\n**State**: closed  \\n\\n**Branch**: `fix/otelgin-data-race` → `main`  \\n\\n**Changes**: +83 / -10 across 2 files  \\n\\n**Labels**: —  \\n\\n**Milestone**: —\\n\\n---\\n\\n## The Performance Argument is Backwards\\n\\nThe current code with the defer is slower than the fix:\\n\\n  Current code overhead:\\n\\n  - Allocates closure for defer\\n\\n  - Saves context reference\\n\\n  - Executes deferred function on return\\n\\n  - Calls WithContext() twice (once to set, once to \\\"restore\\\")\\n\\n  Fixed code:\\n\\n  - No defer\\n\\n  - No closure allocation\\n\\n  - One fewer WithContext() call\\n\\n  - No unnecessary context copy\\n\\n Benchmark comparison (removing defer should show improvement):\\n\\n  -  Current: ~3 allocations per request\\n\\n  -  Fixed:   ~2 allocations per request\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"label mileston the defer provid no benefit let s analyz what the current code doe go savedctx c request context defer func c request c request withcontext savedctx restor after request complet ctx cfg propag extract savedctx propag headercarri c request header c request c request withcontext ctx set new context c next process request when we return defer run and restor the old context question whi restor the context after the request is complet after c next return the request ha been fulli process gin doesn t reus gin context instanc across request downstream handler may spawn goroutin that read c request context the defer write to c request race with those goroutin read there is no code that benefit from this restor sourc id src pr 07 pull request otelgin remov unneed context save restor to avoid data race open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib 8014 canon url http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib pull 8014 author nicksherron creat 2025 10 14t04 01 24z updat 2025 10 17t10 42 07z collect 2026 07 14t14 35 41z state close branch fix otelgin data race main chang 83 10 across 2 file label mileston the perform argument is backward the current code with the defer is slower than the fix current code overhead alloc closur for defer save context refer execut defer function on return call withcontext twice onc to set onc to restor fix code no defer no closur alloc one fewer withcontext call no unnecessari context copi benchmark comparison remov defer should show improv current 3 alloc per request fix 2 alloc per request\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"0e4329537c0aa36a\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.2872744098483038, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.09202387121186258, \"vector_similarity\": 0.742859, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"=====================================\\n\\n+ Hits       11598   11605    +7     \\n\\n+ Misses      2808    2795   -13     \\n\\n  Partials     354     354           \\n\\n```\\n\\n... and [1 file with indirect coverage changes](https://app.codecov.io/gh/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/pull/8014/indirect-changes?src=pr&el=tree-more&utm_medium=referral&utm_source=github&utm_content=comment&utm_campaign=pr+comments&utm_term=open-telemetry)\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary> :rocket: New features to boost your workflow: </summary>\\n\\n- :snowflake: [Test Analytics](https://docs.codecov.com/docs/test-analytics): Detect flaky tests, report on failures, and find test suite problems.\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n---\\n\\n**dmathieu** — 2025-10-14T07:54:22Z\\n\\nThe intent of the defer is to remove the span from the context, so spans using the same context aren't childs of the created span once the middleware has finished running.\\n\\nImaging the following setup:\\n\\n```golang\\n\\nr := gin.New()\\n\\nr.Use(myCustomMiddleware)\\n\\nr.Use(otelgin.Middleware(\\\"my-server\\\"))\\n\\nr.GET(\\\"/\\\", func(c *gin.Context) {\\n\\n\\tc.HTML(http.StatusOK, \\\"index.tmpl\\\", gin.H{})\\n\\n})\\n\\n```\\n\\nWhen `myCustomMiddleware` runs, the span has either not started yet, or is already finished.\\n\\nIf it's already finished, the context shouldn't have the span within it, as we're not within the trace anymore.\\n\\nRight now, if you create a span within `myCustomMiddleware`, after the call to `c.Next()`, it won't be a child of the otelgin span.\\n\\nAfter your change it is one.\\n\\nThis is what the defer prevents.\\n\\nWhile I agree to fixing this race condition, the defer does have a use and I don't think we can just drop it.\\n\\n(ideally, we should add a test for this too)\\n\\n---\\n\\n**akats7** — 2025-10-14T08:01:45Z\\n\\n> The intent of the defer is to remove the span from the context, so spans using the same context aren't childs of the created span once the middleware has finished running.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> Imaging the following setup:\\n\\n> \\n\\n> ```go\\n\\n> r := gin.New()\\n\\n> r.Use(myCustomMiddleware)\\n\\n> r.Use(otelgin.Middleware(\\\"my-server\\\"))\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"hit 11598 11605 7 miss 2808 2795 13 partial 354 354 and 1 file with indirect coverag chang http app codecov io gh open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib pull 8014 indirect chang src pr el tree more utm _ medium referr utm _ sourc github utm _ content comment utm _ campaign pr comment utm _ term open telemetri detail detail summari rocket new featur to boost your workflow summari snowflak test analyt http doc codecov com doc test analyt detect flaki test report on failur and find test suit problem detail dmathieu 2025 10 14t07 54 22z the intent of the defer is to remov the span from the context so span use the same context aren t child of the creat span onc the middlewar ha finish run imag the follow setup golang r gin new r use mycustommiddlewar r use otelgin middlewar my server r get func c gin context c html http statusok index tmpl gin h when mycustommiddlewar run the span ha either not start yet or is alreadi finish if it s alreadi finish the context shouldn t have the span within it a we re not within the trace anymor right now if you creat a span within mycustommiddlewar after the call to c next it won t be a child of the otelgin span after your chang it is one this is what the defer prevent while i agre to fix this race condit the defer doe have a use and i don t think we can just drop it ideal we should add a test for this too akats7 2025 10 14t08 01 45z the intent of the defer is to remov the span from the context so span use the same context aren t child of the creat span onc the middlewar ha finish run imag the follow setup go r gin new r use mycustommiddlewar r use otelgin middlewar my server\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"dfdecac4ba6df693\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.28596376555126585, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.07447096793037976, \"vector_similarity\": 0.77944696, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"  These fixes were accepted because data races are bugs, not \\\"concurrency mechanisms left to users.\\\"\\n\\n**Source ID**: [SRC-PR-07]\\n\\n# Pull Request: otelgin: remove unneeded context save/restore to avoid data race (open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib#8014)\\n\\n**Canonical URL**: https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/pull/8014  \\n\\n**Author**: nicksherron  \\n\\n**Created**: 2025-10-14T04:01:24Z · **Updated**: 2025-10-17T10:42:07Z  \\n\\n**Collected**: 2026-07-14T14:35:41Z  \\n\\n**State**: closed  \\n\\n**Branch**: `fix/otelgin-data-race` → `main`  \\n\\n**Changes**: +83 / -10 across 2 files  \\n\\n**Labels**: —  \\n\\n**Milestone**: —\\n\\n---\\n\\n## The Race Is Real and Reproducible\\n\\n  From issue #4588:\\n\\n  WARNING: DATA RACE\\n\\n  Write at 0x... by goroutine X:\\n\\n    [defer in Middleware]\\n\\n  Read at 0x... by goroutine Y:\\n\\n    [user handler accessing context]\\n\\n  This is undefined behavior. The Go memory model states: \\\"Programs that modify data being simultaneously accessed by multiple goroutines must serialize such access.\\\"\\n\\n**Source ID**: [SRC-PR-07]\\n\\n# Pull Request: otelgin: remove unneeded context save/restore to avoid data race (open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib#8014)\\n\\n**Canonical URL**: https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/pull/8014  \\n\\n**Author**: nicksherron  \\n\\n**Created**: 2025-10-14T04:01:24Z · **Updated**: 2025-10-17T10:42:07Z  \\n\\n**Collected**: 2026-07-14T14:35:41Z  \\n\\n**State**: closed  \\n\\n**Branch**: `fix/otelgin-data-race` → `main`  \\n\\n**Changes**: +83 / -10 across 2 files  \\n\\n**Labels**: —  \\n\\n**Milestone**: —\\n\\n---\\n\\n## The Fix is Correct\\n\\n  The fix simply removes dead code:\\n\\n```go\\n\\n  // Before: Save and restore (unnecessary)\\n\\n  savedCtx := c.Request.Context()\\n\\n  defer func() { c.Request = c.Request.WithContext(savedCtx) }()\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"these fix were accept becaus data race are bug not concurr mechan left to user sourc id src pr 07 pull request otelgin remov unneed context save restor to avoid data race open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib 8014 canon url http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib pull 8014 author nicksherron creat 2025 10 14t04 01 24z updat 2025 10 17t10 42 07z collect 2026 07 14t14 35 41z state close branch fix otelgin data race main chang 83 10 across 2 file label mileston the race is real and reproduc from issu 4588 warn data race write at 0x by goroutin x defer in middlewar read at 0x by goroutin y user handler access context this is undefin behavior the go memori model state program that modifi data be simultan access by multipl goroutin must serial such access sourc id src pr 07 pull request otelgin remov unneed context save restor to avoid data race open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib 8014 canon url http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib pull 8014 author nicksherron creat 2025 10 14t04 01 24z updat 2025 10 17t10 42 07z collect 2026 07 14t14 35 41z state close branch fix otelgin data race main chang 83 10 across 2 file label mileston the fix is correct the fix simpli remov dead code go befor save and restor unnecessari savedctx c request context defer func c request c request withcontext savedctx\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"b40b58fd0d2fea42\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.282798369470081, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.08149212924297289, \"vector_similarity\": 0.75251293, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"I also disagree that folks should do this themselves. Especially since this has been the default behavior for many years now (and the same behavior exists for other instrumentations, such as otelecho).\\n\\nI think this discussion would be better synchronously though. If you're able to, I'd recommend you to join our next SIG meeting (tomorrow at 4pm UTC).\\n\\nhttps://github.com/open-telemetry/community/?tab=readme-ov-file#implementation-sigs\\n\\n---\\n\\n**nicksherron** — 2025-10-14T08:48:08Z\\n\\n> I disagree with this being a violation of Go's conventions (in the context of the middleware pattern, we don't flow forward. At the end of a middleware, we go back to its parent). I also disagree that folks should do this themselves. Especially since this has been the default behavior for many years now (and the same behavior exists for other instrumentations, such as otelecho).\\n\\n> \\n\\n> I think this discussion would be better synchronously though. If you're able to, I'd recommend you to join our next SIG meeting (tomorrow at 4pm UTC). https://github.com/open-telemetry/community/?tab=readme-ov-file#implementation-sigs\\n\\nOk well one thing is for certain, you all have a much better understanding of the existing patterns and user expectations for this project than I do. It's getting quite late for me so I'll sleep on this and give your points their time to marinate. As far as the SIG meeting goes that's a great idea (and sounds fun too) - unfortunately that's a fairly busy time for me but I'll do my best to make it.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**nicksherron** — 2025-10-14T08:59:49Z\\n\\n> Especially since this has been the default behavior for many years now (and the same behavior exists for other instrumentations, such as otelecho).\\n\\nThis is a strong argument, I didn’t realize that was the case.  I wasn’t looking to add a breaking change and I’m glad you pointed it out. I’ll see what I can do to come up with something that maintains the existing semantics while also fixing the race.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**dmathieu** — 2025-10-17T10:41:58Z\\n\\nWe have been discussing this in yesterday's SIG meeting, and have decided this fix is incorrect and the current behavior is the proper one.\\n\\nSee https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-go-contrib/issues/4588#issuecomment-3414917592\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"i also disagre that folk should do this themselv especi sinc this ha been the default behavior for mani year now and the same behavior exist for other instrument such a otelecho i think this discuss would be better synchron though if you re abl to i d recommend you to join our next sig meet tomorrow at 4pm utc http github com open telemetri communiti tab readm ov file implement sig nicksherron 2025 10 14t08 48 08z i disagre with this be a violat of go s convent in the context of the middlewar pattern we don t flow forward at the end of a middlewar we go back to it parent i also disagre that folk should do this themselv especi sinc this ha been the default behavior for mani year now and the same behavior exist for other instrument such a otelecho i think this discuss would be better synchron though if you re abl to i d recommend you to join our next sig meet tomorrow at 4pm utc http github com open telemetri communiti tab readm ov file implement sig ok well one thing is for certain you all have a much better understand of the exist pattern and user expect for this project than i do it s get quit late for me so i ll sleep on this and give your point their time to marin a far a the sig meet go that s a great idea and sound fun too unfortun that s a fair busi time for me but i ll do my best to make it nicksherron 2025 10 14t08 59 49z especi sinc this ha been the default behavior for mani year now and the same behavior exist for other instrument such a otelecho this is a strong argument i didn t realiz that wa the case i wasn t look to add a break chang and i m glad you point it out i ll see what i can do to come up with someth that maintain the exist semant while also fix the race dmathieu 2025 10 17t10 41 58z we have been discuss this in yesterday s sig meet and have decid this fix is incorrect and the current behavior is the proper one see http github com open telemetri opentelemetri go contrib issu 4588 issuecom 3414917592\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"b87d58f69aed9a06\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.281982240470081, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.08149212924297287, \"vector_similarity\": 0.7497925, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"> \\t\\n\\n> r.GET(\\\"/\\\", func(c *gin.Context) {\\n\\n> \\tc.HTML(http.StatusOK, \\\"index.tmpl\\\", gin.H{})\\n\\n> })\\n\\n> ```\\n\\n> \\n\\n> When `myCustomMiddleware` runs, the span has either not started yet, or is already finished. If it's already finished, the context shouldn't have the span within it, as we're not within the trace anymore.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> Right now, if you create a span within `myCustomMiddleware`, after the call to `c.Next()`, it won't be a child of the otelgin span. After your change it is one.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> This is what the defer prevents. While I agree to fixing this race condition, the defer does have a use and I don't think we can just drop it.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> (ideally, we should add a test for this too)\\n\\nAh that’s a really good observation\\n\\n---\\n\\n**nicksherron** — 2025-10-14T08:34:20Z\\n\\n> The intent of the defer is to remove the span from the context, so spans using the same context aren't childs of the created span once the middleware has finished running.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> Imaging the following setup:\\n\\n> \\n\\n> ```go\\n\\n> r := gin.New()\\n\\n> r.Use(myCustomMiddleware)\\n\\n> r.Use(otelgin.Middleware(\\\"my-server\\\"))\\n\\n> \\t\\n\\n> r.GET(\\\"/\\\", func(c *gin.Context) {\\n\\n> \\tc.HTML(http.StatusOK, \\\"index.tmpl\\\", gin.H{})\\n\\n> })\\n\\n> ```\\n\\n> \\n\\n> When `myCustomMiddleware` runs, the span has either not started yet, or is already finished. If it's already finished, the context shouldn't have the span within it, as we're not within the trace anymore.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> Right now, if you create a span within `myCustomMiddleware`, after the call to `c.Next()`, it won't be a child of the otelgin span. After your change it is one.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> This is what the defer prevents. While I agree to fixing this race condition, the defer does have a use and I don't think we can just drop it.\\n\\n> \\n\\n> (ideally, we should add a test for this too)\\n\\nThat's a really good theory about what the original code was intending to do and you made a really good point. However, I think there are some fundamental issues with this approach:\\n\\n1. This is an anti-pattern for context.Context\\n\\nThe defer modifies c.Request.Context() after passing it forward, which violates Go's context conventions. From the https://pkg.go.dev/context:\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"r get func c gin context c html http statusok index tmpl gin h when mycustommiddlewar run the span ha either not start yet or is alreadi finish if it s alreadi finish the context shouldn t have the span within it a we re not within the trace anymor right now if you creat a span within mycustommiddlewar after the call to c next it won t be a child of the otelgin span after your chang it is one this is what the defer prevent while i agre to fix this race condit the defer doe have a use and i don t think we can just drop it ideal we should add a test for this too ah that s a realli good observ nicksherron 2025 10 14t08 34 20z the intent of the defer is to remov the span from the context so span use the same context aren t child of the creat span onc the middlewar ha finish run imag the follow setup go r gin new r use mycustommiddlewar r use otelgin middlewar my server r get func c gin context c html http statusok index tmpl gin h when mycustommiddlewar run the span ha either not start yet or is alreadi finish if it s alreadi finish the context shouldn t have the span within it a we re not within the trace anymor right now if you creat a span within mycustommiddlewar after the call to c next it won t be a child of the otelgin span after your chang it is one this is what the defer prevent while i agre to fix this race condit the defer doe have a use and i don t think we can just drop it ideal we should add a test for this too that s a realli good theori about what the origin code wa intend to do and you made a realli good point howev i think there are some fundament issu with this approach 1 this is an anti pattern for context context the defer modifi c request context after pass it forward which violat go s context convent from the http pkg go dev context\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"89d4bd453e9caf0c\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.2782537326324506, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.06744980661778663, \"vector_similarity\": 0.77012956, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"type\": \"doc\", \"size\": 33019, \"chunk_count\": 21, \"create_date\": \"2026-07-28T23:53:51\", \"update_date\": \"2026-07-31T20:10:16\", \"token_count\": 10517, \"thumbnail\": \"\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"meta_fields\": {\"path\": \"sources\"}}}, {\"content\": \"  ctx := cfg.Propagators.Extract(savedCtx, ...)\\n\\n  // After: Use context directly (correct)\\n\\n  reqCtx := c.Request.Context()\\n\\n  ctx := cfg.Propagators.Extract(reqCtx, ...)\\n\\n```\\n\\n  The behavior is identical except:\\n\\n  -  No race condition\\n\\n  -  Better performance\\n\\n  -  Simpler code\\n\\n  -  Tests pass with -race\\n\\n  Conclusion\\n\\n  This is a straightforward bug fix that:\\n\\n  1. Removes unnecessary code that causes a race\\n\\n  2. Improves performance (fewer allocations, no defer overhead)\\n\\n  3. Fixes a 2-year-old issue (#4588)\\n\\n  4. Follows precedent from previous race fixes in this project\\n\\n  5. Enables CI to enforce race-free code\\n\\n  There is no downside to this change, and significant benefits. I respectfully request approval to merge.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**akats7** — 2025-10-14T04:58:07Z\\n\\nHi @nicksherron, \\n\\nIt's a fair question, I also don't really see the benefit of it, I'm going to assume it was added for hygiene to avoid leaking the modifications out of the intended request's middleware chain.\\n\\n@flc1125 what are your thoughts, even if it doesn't provide thread safety it does seem valid\\n\\n---\\n\\n**flc1125** — 2025-10-14T05:01:16Z\\n\\n> I'm going to assume it was added for hygiene to avoid leaking the modifications out of the intended request's middleware chain.\\n\\nI agree with this situation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**nicksherron** — 2025-10-14T05:06:09Z\\n\\nI'm confused about this \\\"doesn't provide thread safety\\\" claim. I'm starring at a test failure due to data race when running `go test -race`  in one of my projects that clearly points the finger to the code I removed as being the culprit. When I do a `go mod -replace` using my change in this PR the race goes away. \\n\\n---\\n\\n**flc1125** — 2025-10-14T05:09:52Z\\n\\n> I'm confused about this \\\"doesn't provide thread safety\\\" claim. I'm starring at a test failure due to data race when running `go test -race` in one of my projects that clearly points the finger to the code I removed as being the culprit. When I do a `go mod -replace` using my change in this PR the race goes away.\\n\", \"content_ltks\": \"ctx cfg propag extract savedctx after use context direct correct reqctx c request context ctx cfg propag extract reqctx the behavior is ident except no race condit better perform simpler code test pas with race conclus this is a straightforward bug fix that 1 remov unnecessari code that caus a race 2 improv perform fewer alloc no defer overhead 3 fix a2 year old issu 4588 4 follow preced from previous race fix in this project 5 enabl ci to enforc race free code there is no downsid to this chang and signific benefit i respect request approv to merg akats7 2025 10 14t04 58 07z hi nicksherron it s a fair question i also don t realli see the benefit of it i m go to assum it wa ad for hygien to avoid leak the modif out of the intend request s middlewar chain flc1125 what are your thought even if it doesn t provid thread safeti it doe seem valid flc1125 2025 10 14t05 01 16z i m go to assum it wa ad for hygien to avoid leak the modif out of the intend request s middlewar chain i agre with this situat nicksherron 2025 10 14t05 06 09z i m confus about this doesn t provid thread safeti claim i m star at a test failur due to data race when run go test race in one of my project that clear point the finger to the code i remov a be the culprit when i do a go mod replac use my chang in this pr the race go away flc1125 2025 10 14t05 09 52z i m confus about this doesn t provid thread safeti claim i m star at a test failur due to data race when run go test race in one of my project that clear point the finger to the code i remov a be the culprit when i do a go mod replac use my chang in this pr the race go away\", \"dataset_id\": \"74f29ea88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"doc_type_kwd\": \"text\", \"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"document_keyword\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"id\": \"cad80d2ecd6e532f\", \"image_id\": \"\", \"important_keywords\": [], \"mom_id\": \"\", \"positions\": [], \"row_id\": null, \"similarity\": 0.27724725047008103, \"tag_kwd\": [], \"term_similarity\": 0.08149212924297287, \"vector_similarity\": 0.7340092, \"dataset_name\": \"C2Raw-2\", \"document_name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"document_metadata\": {\"document_id\": \"8753acb88a9c11f197db2303d7337e37\", \"name\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014.md\", \"location\": \"opentelemetry/pulls/opentelemetry-go-contrib-8014